--TEST--
`var` writes no type, so a declaration with no initializer is refused at the missing `=` — `rule:types/var-inference`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `var` writes no type, so the initializer is the only thing a type can be
// read off and the parser requires it where it would be. The accepted
// spelling is first, so a `var` that stopped inferring at all fails here as
// a missing line rather than reading as a stricter `var`.
var $n = 1;
echo $n, "\n";

var $x;
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0101]: expected `=` — `var` infers its type from the initializer, so one is required
  --> case.nvs:9:7
%A
error[E0102]: expected an expression
  --> case.nvs:9:7
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
